question_text:
What is the output of the following C++ code?

```cpp
int x = 7;
if (x = 0)
    cout << "A";
else if (x == 7)
    cout << "B";
else
    cout << "C";
```

What is printed by the following C++ code?

```cpp
int x = 8, y = 3;

if (x > 5)
    if (y > 5)
        cout << "A";
    else
        cout << "B";
else
    cout << "C";
```

A programmer intends to print `Valid` only when 0 < x < 10. What does this program actually print?

```cpp
int x = 50;

if (0 < x < 10)
    cout << "Valid";
else
    cout << "Invalid";
```

What happens when the following code is compiled and executed?

```cpp
int score = 40;

if (score >= 60);
    cout << "Pass";
else
    cout << "Fail";
```

Predict the exact output of this program.

```cpp
int n = 2;

switch (n) {
    case 1:
        cout << "A";
    case 2:
        cout << "B";
    case 3:
        cout << "C";
        break;
    default:
        cout << "D";
}
```

What is wrong with the following program?

```cpp
int choice = 4;

switch (choice) {
    case 2 + 2:
        cout << "A";
        break;
    case 4:
        cout << "B";
        break;
    default:
        cout << "C";
}
```

What is the output of the following code?

```cpp
int a = 7, b = 2;
double result = (a > b) ? a / b : b / a;
cout << result;
```

What is printed by this code?

```cpp
int x = 5;
int y = 8;
int z = 0;

z = x > y ? x : y == 8 ? 20 : 30;
cout << z;
```

The programmer wants scores from 90 through 100 to receive `A`, scores from 80 through 89 to receive `B`, and everything below 80 to receive `C`. Which statement best describes the bug in this code?

```cpp
int score = 90;

if (score > 90 && score <= 100)
    cout << "A";
else if (score >= 80 && score < 90)
    cout << "B";
else
    cout << "C";
```

What is the exact output of the following program?

```cpp
int x = 0;
int y = 4;

if (x != 0 && y / x > 2)
    cout << "A";
else if (x == 0 || ++y > 4)
    cout << y;
else
    cout << "C";
```
